## Broader context
Photovoltaic technology offers a sustainable solution to soaring global energy demands. The domination of crystalline silicon solar cells is expected to ease in the foreseeable future as they approach their theoretical efficiency limit. Metal-halide perovskite solar cells have emerged as the fastest-developing new photovoltaic technology rivalling crystalline silicon, largely due to its compatibility with large-scale production using conventional printing processes. The transition from research to industrial applications of perovskite solar cells requires further advancements in both operational stability and large-area device fabrication; two factors that often display an inverse inter-dependency. The present work demonstrates that incorporation of certain benzenethiol derivatives at the interface between the perovskite light absorber layer and the layer of hole-transporting material provides the dual benefit of improvement in the band-energy alignment and also protection of the perovskite from environmental degradation. A key feature of this work is the use of cm-sized devices rather than the mm-scale solar cells commonly employed for research purposes. The simple approach developed here enables fabrication of larger cells with characteristics that are very similar to those of smaller devices.
